  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Less than 15 km from Nice, Mont Chauve d'Aspremont rises to 870 metres and offers an open view over the Baie des Anges, the Var valley and the surrounding hills. The summit bears a disused fort built in 1885-1887 (occupied notably by EDF, civil aviation and the fire brigade according to the Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office), which is not open to visitors, and telecommunications installations, a striking contrast with the scrubland landscape crossed on the way up.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The tour of Mont Chauve, starting from the village of Aspremont, covers about 7.5 km with roughly 345 metres of elevation gain over a 3-hour duration, rated easy. The trail follows the GR5/GR51 markers for a good part of the route, before reaching the area around the fort. Neither the Nice Côte d'Azur tourist office nor the commune publishes a dog rule for this trail: as a precaution, keep your dog on a leash, especially near the fort.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the summit is very windy, even in fine weather — bring a light layer for yourself and something to shelter your dog from the wind during the picnic stop.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The village of Aspremont, with its stepped lanes typical of the hilltop villages inland from Nice, is easy to visit before or after the hike.</p>
    </div>

